-We give a quantum algorithm for the hidden subgroup problem in finite $p$-groups $G$ of nilpotency class $c < p$, for an arbitrary hidden subgroup $H$, with $G$ given by the structure constants of its Lazard Lie algebra $\mathcal{L} \cong \Fp^m$. For every fixed $c$, with the collision step of Ivanyos and Santha's diagonal-equation solver as an imported theorem, it has bounded error and runs in time polynomial in $m$, $\log p$, the label width and $\log(1/\eps)$. Corollaries give the same guarantee for black-box $p$-groups of class $c < p$ with unique encoding and, with Imran and Ivanyos's exact algorithm for primes $p \le c$, for black-box nilpotent groups of fixed class with a standard oracle. Beyond class two, the degree-by-degree elimination of Ivanyos and Santha needs automorphisms that preserve $H$ and scale the centre, and public ones need not exist: the class-three Dixmier--Lister algebra has no positive grading over $\Fp$ ($p > 3$), and its automorphisms scale its centre only by $\pm1$. We instead query $f$ at $c$ distinct nonzero evaluations of the graded cover $\A = \bigoplus_{k=1}^c t^k\gamma_k(\mathcal{L})$. The resulting oracle, built without knowing $H$, hides the homogenization of $\log H$ in the cover, which every graded dilation preserves; fewer evaluations do not suffice for every $H$. The descent then removes the grades $c,\dots,2$, and abelian Fourier sampling on grade one recovers $\log H$. In the same model, for $m \ge 2$, the classical randomized query complexity is $\Theta(p^{(m-1)/2})$. The algebra of the cover is standard; what is new is its use to make an unknown hidden subgroup dilation-invariant. The elimination is that of Ivanyos and Santha, the query separation is not new, the exponent of $m$ is $\Omega(c^2)$, and no application is known. All proofs have been checked only by AI and await human review.
+We give a quantum algorithm for the hidden subgroup problem in finite $p$-groups $G$ of nilpotency class $c < p$, for an arbitrary hidden subgroup $H$, with $G$ given by the structure constants of its Lazard Lie algebra $\mathcal{L} \cong \Fp^m$. For every fixed $c$, with the collision step of Ivanyos and Santha's diagonal-equation solver as an imported theorem, it has bounded error and runs in time polynomial in $m$, $\log p$, the label width and $\log(1/\eps)$. Corollaries give the same guarantee for black-box $p$-groups of class $c < p$ with unique encoding and, with Imran and Ivanyos's exact algorithm for primes $p \le c$, for black-box nilpotent groups of fixed class with a standard oracle. Beyond class two, the degree-by-degree elimination of Ivanyos and Santha needs automorphisms that preserve $H$ and scale the centre, and public ones need not exist: the class-three Dixmier--Lister algebra has no positive grading over $\Fp$ ($p > 3$), and its automorphisms scale its centre only by $\pm1$. We instead query $f$ at $c$ distinct nonzero evaluations of the graded cover $\A = \bigoplus_{k=1}^c t^k\gamma_k(\mathcal{L})$. The resulting oracle, built without knowing $H$, hides the homogenization of $\log H$ in the cover, which every graded dilation preserves; fewer evaluations do not suffice for every $H$. The descent then removes the grades $c,\dots,2$, and abelian Fourier sampling on grade one recovers $\log H$. In the same model, for $m \ge 2$, the classical randomized query complexity is $\Theta(p^{(m-1)/2})$. The algebra of the cover is standard; what is new is its use to make an unknown hidden subgroup dilation-invariant. The elimination is that of Ivanyos and Santha, the query separation is not new, the exponent of $m$ is $\Omega(c^2)$ (at most $\ReesClassNineHalvedExp$ at $c = 9$), and no application is known. All proofs have been checked only by AI and await human review.
