 \paragraph{Black-box groups and other presentations.} Definition~\ref{def:input-contract} asks for the structure constants of the Lazard Lie algebra. Section~\ref{sec:black-box} shows that unitriangular matrix groups and consistent power-commutator presentations reduce to it, and that so do black-box groups. For a black-box $p$-group of class $c<p$ with unique encoding and a coherent multiplication oracle, Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box} (exponent $p$) and Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe} (any exponent) acquire an explicit chart with no query to $f$, and then run Theorem~\ref{thm:main}. Like Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, both are conditional only on Theorem~\ref{thm:is-import}, and their query counts are polynomial in the encoding length and $\log(1/\eps)$ and independent of the number of generators. Above exponent $p$ the route is the reduction of Ivanyos, Sanselme and Santha~\cite[Theorem~2]{IvanyosSanselmeSantha2012}, which we do not claim as new (Remark~\ref{rem:black-box-pe-source}). The main consequence is Corollary~\ref{cor:nilpotent}: with a Sylow split and the exact algorithm of Imran and Ivanyos~\cite[Theorem~1]{ImranIvanyos2023} for the primes $p \le c$, the same guarantee holds for every black-box nilpotent group of fixed class with unique encoding, whatever the primes dividing its order. Imran and Ivanyos note that an efficient, even randomized, zero-sum algorithm would give a probabilistic algorithm for nilpotent groups of smooth order and bounded class, in their model, where $H$ is given by an oracle that creates a purification of the subgroup state~\cite[Section~6 and Proposition~3]{ImranIvanyos2023}. Corollary~\ref{cor:nilpotent} gives such an algorithm by another route, for every order, when $H$ is given by a standard oracle $U_f$, conditional on Theorem~\ref{thm:is-import}; it does not cover their more general oracle, and it leaves the zero-sum question open (Remark~\ref{rem:nilpotent-scope}). Corollary~\ref{cor:nilpotent} also gives, for each fixed $n \