 \paragraph{Direct twists beyond class two.} For a graded $\mathcal{L}$ obstacle~(1) is absent, and one may ask whether the class-two twist of~\cite{IvanyosSanselmeSantha2012} extends without a cover. Two observations, argued and not proved here, suggest that it does not in any simple way. First, a precise form of the remark of~\cite[Section~1]{ImranIvanyos2023} holds: if the abelian layer that is measured and the twisting automorphisms are fixed without knowledge of $H$, and the method must work for every cyclic $H$, then beyond class two, except on runs of probability $O(1/p)$ per copy, the twists that are used act trivially on $\gamma_c(\mathcal{L})$, so the cancellation reduces to the binary zero-sum weights of~\cite{ImranIvanyos2023}. Second, automorphisms that stabilize $H\gamma_c(G)$ and scale the centre exist in every graded example we checked, but they depend on $H$, and finding $H\gamma_c(G)$ is itself the problem: for $|H| = p$, a hiding procedure for the abelian group $H\gamma_c(G)$ yields $H$ by abelian Fourier sampling inside it, and the oracle supplies none. The dilations of the cover preserve the lifted subgroup for every $H$, and so supply the quotient coset states without knowing $H$. We do not claim that the cover is necessary for graded $\mathcal{L}$. The arguments and the exact checks behind both observations are in the research notes listed in Appendix~\ref{app:artifacts}.
 
 \paragraph{What is new, and what is not.} The result is Theorem~\ref{thm:main}: for every fixed class $c$, a polynomial-time bounded-error quantum algorithm for an arbitrary hidden subgroup in Lazard $p$-groups of class $c<p$, with $p$ growing, conditional on Theorem~\ref{thm:is-import}. Its main consequence is Corollary~\ref{cor:nilpotent}, for black-box nilpotent groups of fixed class with unique encoding, which contains, for a standard oracle, the smooth-order case that~\cite[Section~6]{ImranIvanyos2023} relate to their zero-sum question. Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is its only new ingredient: the reduction above exponent $p$ is that of~\cite[Theorem~2]{IvanyosSanselmeSantha2012} (Remark~\ref{rem:black-box-pe-source}), the primes $p \le c$ are those of~\cite[Theorem~1]{ImranIvanyos2023}, and the Sylow split is elementary. Imran and Ivanyos note that the automorphisms behind the class-two algorithms do not exist in general beyond class two~\cite[Section~1]{ImranIvanyos2023}, and we are not aware of an earlier polynomial-time algorithm for arbitrary hidden subgroups at fixed class three or more with $p$ growing, outside groups with an abelian subgroup of index $p$, where earlier results combine (Section~\ref{sec:related}).
